WEBVTT

q0001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.788
Why can't four honest numbers be averaged into one honest winner?

q0002
00:00:05.788 --> 00:00:14.880
This episode has one job: Workload, output contract, machine, reuse, and measurement boundary define a claim's scope; similar-looking ratios outside that scope cannot be pooled into one conclusion.

q0003
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:23.972
We build on How to read a CM/comparator ratio; their definitions stay fixed while this lesson adds a new layer.

q0004
00:00:23.972 --> 00:00:33.064
Scope names the conditions under which a claim is supported; the boundary names the work that was measured.

q0005
00:00:33.064 --> 00:00:40.307
Watch for the label scope; it stays attached to the artifact this episode means.

q0006
00:00:40.307 --> 00:00:47.065
When we say workload, the highlight identifies its layer before we interpret it.

q0007
00:00:47.065 --> 00:00:55.763
The term output contract receives its own visual state so it cannot drift into a neighboring concept.

q0008
00:00:55.763 --> 00:01:02.036
We will keep incomparable boundary visible whenever its definition controls the inference.

q0009
00:01:02.036 --> 00:01:11.128
The next sequence uses Small AND/INV cone as a conceptual teaching example, not as measured benchmark evidence.

q0010
00:01:11.128 --> 00:01:15.462
Our stable example is Small AND/INV cone.

q0011
00:01:15.462 --> 00:01:24.554
A conceptual five-input AND/INV cone whose output has four-variable semantic support, paired later with separately labeled retained EPFL evidence.

q0012
00:01:24.554 --> 00:01:30.827
Teach cone support, fanout, lowering, exact digests, and the accepted EPFL mechanism.

q0013
00:01:30.827 --> 00:01:38.555
We now construct the Small AND/INV cone and name every part before interpreting it.

q0014
00:01:38.555 --> 00:01:46.768
Place B2/B4 kernel, EPFL kernel, preparation, and wrapper panels on a two-axis workload/boundary grid.

q0015
00:01:46.768 --> 00:01:53.526
Attempt to pool them, surface the mismatches, and return to separate scoped conclusions.

q0016
00:01:53.526 --> 00:01:58.345
Now isolate scope comparison inside Small AND/INV cone.

q0017
00:01:58.345 --> 00:02:06.558
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for scope comparison changes.

q0018
00:02:06.558 --> 00:02:14.771
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to scope comparison, not to a substituted workload.

q0019
00:02:14.771 --> 00:02:21.044
Allowed wording updates as the viewer drags a scope bracket across cells.

q0020
00:02:21.044 --> 00:02:25.863
Now isolate non-pooling rule inside Small AND/INV cone.

q0021
00:02:25.863 --> 00:02:34.076
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for non-pooling rule changes.

q0022
00:02:34.076 --> 00:02:42.289
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to non-pooling rule, not to a substituted workload.

q0023
00:02:42.289 --> 00:02:48.562
Current evidence does not support a universal claim that CM beats CSE-flat.

q0024
00:02:48.562 --> 00:02:53.381
Read that statement only within this scope: cross-study synthesis.

q0025
00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:58.200
Its declared measurement boundary is multiple boundaries kept separate.

q0026
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.019
The uncertainty field says scopes are intentionally not pooled.

q0027
00:03:03.019 --> 00:03:12.111
Every ratio must state numerator, denominator, workload, measurement boundary, and uncertainty; below one favors CM only for CM/comparator.

q0028
00:03:12.111 --> 00:03:17.414
Read that statement only within this scope: video evidence contract.

q0029
00:03:17.414 --> 00:03:21.748
Its declared measurement boundary is all numeric scenes.

q0030
00:03:21.748 --> 00:03:25.597
The uncertainty field says declared per ratio.

q0031
00:03:25.597 --> 00:03:34.689
CM, CM-IR, SAT, BDD, minimization, and symbolic algebra answer different questions and must be compared under matched output contracts.

q0032
00:03:34.689 --> 00:03:40.962
Read that statement only within this scope: implemented backend and task contracts.

q0033
00:03:40.962 --> 00:03:44.811
Its declared measurement boundary is output contract.

q0034
00:03:44.811 --> 00:03:48.175
The uncertainty field says scope dependent.

q0035
00:03:48.175 --> 00:03:57.267
The nearest confusing lesson is Reuse and break-even economics; it owns one-time versus repeated cost, while this episode owns scope comparison.

q0036
00:03:57.267 --> 00:04:04.995
The cone starts from five named inputs, but the displayed output has four-variable semantic support.

q0037
00:04:04.995 --> 00:04:10.298
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate scope comparison.

q0038
00:04:10.298 --> 00:04:17.541
The inert input stays outlined so nominal width cannot be mistaken for active support.

q0039
00:04:17.541 --> 00:04:22.844
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate non-pooling rule.

q0040
00:04:22.844 --> 00:04:30.087
A topological trace orders the gates without changing their connectivity or the output function.

q0041
00:04:30.087 --> 00:04:37.330
B2/B4, EPFL, preparation, kernel, and wrapper evidence remain separate when their scopes differ.

q0042
00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:44.573
Reject the two results that cannot be pooled with a declared kernel/workload cell.

q0043
00:04:44.573 --> 00:04:48.907
Honest evidence gets narrower before it gets stronger.

q0044
00:04:48.907 --> 00:04:53.726
Now isolate boundary mismatch inside Small AND/INV cone.

q0045
00:04:53.726 --> 00:05:01.939
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for boundary mismatch changes.

q0046
00:05:01.939 --> 00:05:10.152
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to boundary mismatch, not to a substituted workload.

q0047
00:05:10.152 --> 00:05:16.910
A pooling funnel rejects cards with mismatched output, boundary, machine, or dependence structure.

q0048
00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:25.608
AND and inversion nodes form a small exact dependency cone whose fanout remains visible throughout the lesson.

q0049
00:05:25.608 --> 00:05:30.911
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate boundary mismatch.

q0050
00:05:30.911 --> 00:05:38.639
A common mistake is this: All CM/comparator ratios estimate one underlying universal performance number.

q0051
00:05:38.639 --> 00:05:43.942
Repair it by returning to the owned distinction: scope comparison.

q0053
00:05:46.973 --> 00:05:54.701
Use the episode's central distinction to answer: Honest evidence gets narrower before it gets stronger.

q0054
00:05:54.701 --> 00:06:00.004
Keep each result inside the conditions that made it true.
